ning at the C ABI level. Since we're passing raw pointers across FFI, the bit pattern is the same regardless of the type annotation. So this should work.\n\nLooking at the existing logup_gkr_input_eval FFI, the Rust side declares the rules parameter as `*const std::ffi::c_void` while CUDA expects `const Rule *d_rules`, but the ABI just sees pointers—the type difference is fine at that level. The real question is whether this holds when the pointer is stored inside a struct that gets uploaded to device memory. The GkrInputScatterCtx struct has the same pointer field on both sides—8 bytes in both cases—so when the struct is copied to the device, the bit pattern remains correct and the CUDA kernel reads the proper pointer value.\n\nNow I'm checking the preprocessed_data pointer handling. The code extracts a pointer to the preprocessed trace buffer, or passes null if there's no preprocessed data. The Rust side gets `*const F` directly while CUDA expects the same, so this should align correctly too. The kernel only accesses `d_preprocessed` when the source type is `ENTRY_PREPROCESSED`, so as long as AIRs without preprocessed data don't have that source type, the actual pointer value is irrelevant—using `std::ptr::null()` would work just as well as the empty buffer's pointer.\n\nNow I'm checking the struct layout between the CUDA and Rust definitions for `GkrInputScatterCtx` to make sure the field ordering and sizes match up correctly. Verifying the struct layouts match between C and Rust — both come to 76 bytes with proper alignment. Now I'm realizing there's a concurrency issue with the scatter temporary buffer: while the lifting loop processes sequentially within the scatter thread, all the non-lifted scatter AIRs are writing directly to their outputs simultaneously, which could create contention or data races I need to think through more carefully. leaves_ptr via the kernel. ALL lifted SCATTER AIRs write to d_scatter_tmp via the kernel. But the kernel is a SINGLE laun